Janis Papanagnou wrote:
> A second opinon; avoid metal armor and use studded leather instead, do
> Elbereth, and go for the low level attack spell; as opposed to throwing
> daggers (which is in principle a good thing for wizards to do) you won't
> erase the engraving if you cast spells. Combine the methods, use dagger
> from distance, engrave before monsters are adjacent, use spells while
> they are close by.

Even casting spells can blow your dust away, but it is less likely.

Don't be 100% sure that you can cast force bolt at an adjacent monster 
and it won't respond.  Also some monsters like say the cubs and pups are 
more dangerous when they are not right next to you.

The contents of my cut & paste buffer is usually: E-y  Elbereth (Pre 
Magicbane.)

If I am standing on two working Elbereth's I'll risk a spell at an 
adjacent beast, three and I'll toss daggers, with four or more I'll 
whack them.  If I don't have a clear shot I'll just keep adding 
engravings until I run out of dust.

I suggest that you bypass the mines and dive for the books and boulder 
training.  You can get to Str 15 or more for the dagger toss and you 
make the Gnome With The Wand That Kills wait until you have both magic 
resistance and reflection.

The mines strongly favors gnomes for generation and those max out at the 
King.  And the Gnome King usually doesn't start with even enough gear to 
cross dress.  The dwarves can be dealt with at range with daggers or 
spells, unless they have crossbows, but they'll never have poisoned bolts.

You are more likely to be mobbed by multiple monster types in the mines 
than in the general dungeon because there are very few doors in the 
mines and the dwarves keep adding new routes to you.

